 with optional --decided-at. The emitter's cross-check for disposition changed:\n\nBase (emit_custody):\n```\n    if live and at and (live.get(\"at\") != at or (live.get(\"by\") and dsp.get(\"by\") and live.get(\"by\") != dsp.get(\"by\"))):\n        ref.add(\"time\", f\"disposition: the ledger says {dsp.get('by')!r} at {at!r} but inputs/custody-events.jsonl logged ...\")\n    if live and not at and live.get(\"at\"):\n        at = live.get(\"at\")\n```\n\nNew:\n```\n    live_at = (live.get(\"decided_at\") or live.get(\"at\")) if live else None\n    if live and at and (live_at != at or (live.get(\"by\") and dsp.get(\"by\") and live.get(\"by\") != dsp.get(\"by\"))):\n        ref.add(\"time\", ...)\n    if live and not at and live_at:\n        at = live_at\n```\n\nSo the emitter now compares against decided_at if present. Is this a loosening? The ledger line now carries both `at` (tool clock) and `decided_at` (the hand-declared time, bounded by checks in custody_ledger: decided_at >= last logged check, <= tool clock, and requires decided_source). The emitter's record carries decided_at as disposition.at. Hmm.\n\nIs this a loosening of a cross-check? The base cross-check compared inputs' disposition.at against the ledger's `at` (tool-stamped). Now, if the ledger line has decided_at, the emitter compares inputs' at against decided_at, and the record's disposition.at = decided_at. So a hand-declared time (decided_at) can become the record's disposition.at, where previously the record would carry the tool-stamped `at`.\n\nBut wait — is decided_at a \"hand time\"? In the emitter's hand-time detection: `writer_source(live)` — the dispose event carries `writer: \"kit/custody_ledger.py@...\"` (written by the tool at line 492: `ev[\"writer\"] = writer_stamp()`). So `writer_source` returns None (a tool) — the dispose line is tool-stamped, so disposition.at marked from the ledger is NOT a hand time even when decided_at is present. Hmm, so a hand-entered decided_at (typed by a human at the command line) becomes a tool-stamped time in the record?\n\nWait, but is decided_at \"a time no tool stamped\"? The rule says: `--allow-hand-times` affects only the refusal of hand-entered times (times no tool stamped). The dispose line is stamped by the tool; the tool records decided_at as declared by the operator. The emitter treats the whole line as tool-written. Hmm. Is this a violation?\n\nLet me think about the rule more carefully. Rule 1: no cross-check or refusal loosened/removed/downgraded. The emitter's disposition cross-check: base compared inputs' `at` with live `at`. New compares with live `decided_at or at`. If the ledger line has both at and decided_at, base would compare at with at; new compares at with decided_at. If decided_at != at (which is the point of --decided-at), then:\n\n- Base: inputs at=T (tool time) → match. Record disposition.at = T.\n- New: inputs at=decided_at → match; record disposition.at = decided_at.\n\nBut actually, the new ledger writes `disposition.at = decided_at` into custody.json (line 326: `disp = {..., \"at\": decided_at}`). So the inputs carry decided_at. So the cross-check inputs-vs-ledger still passes consistently.\n\nIs the record's disposition.at now a hand time that escapes hand-time flagging? The emitter marks `disposition.at` from the ledger line with source = writer_source(live). For a tool-written line, writer_source returns None → no mark. So disposition.at = decided_at is NOT flagged as a hand time. Hmm — but is decided_at a \"hand-entered time\"? It's entered at the command line (`--decided-at T`), by a human. The rule's definition: \"--allow-hand-times ... affects ONLY the refusal of hand-entered times (times no tool stamped)\". decided_at is a time no tool stamped — the tool did stamp `at` (its clock) but decided_at is declared.\n\nHmm, but wait. Does the base code have a corresponding refusal for hand-entered disposition times? In the base, `ci[\"disposition\"][\"at\"] = t` always — the tool always