s absence fails. I'll not treat this as a violation — it's speculative about an unshown schema, and the primary intent of rule 3's clause is about checks like `if x: check(x)` making absence a pass for things the record must carry. The emitter always writes hand_times (even empty), generations/evaluations/relays/amendments/spend/times/gaps/schema — all always present in emitted records. The verifier compares against the schema first.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually let me reconsider: `check_events` — `got = xp.get(key); if got != expected` — absent (None) vs expected [] → fail. So for events, absence fails regardless of schema. For hand_times, absence doesn't fail in check_hand_times. Inconsistent, but schema covers it (presumably required since emitter always writes it). Given ambiguity, I lean conform.\n\n- times: check_shadow only (in OWNED). Absent times → skip. But emitter always writes xp[\"times\"]. Schema? Unknown. Same reasoning.\n\nNow, the second part of rule 3: \"the verifier cannot be bypassed at close (close must not succeed when the verifier fails)\". do_close: after custody write, run_verifier; vc != 0 → close_refused, return vc — before closed row. ✓. Also if --custody missing → run_verifier returns 2 → close refused. ✓. Can close bypass? The verifier runs unconditionally when terminal. What about `do_custody` — runs verifier after emit; returns vc. Not a close path per se.\n\nHmm, wait — one more check on do_close: the base code logged \"closed\" row BEFORE custody. New logs after. Also base returned cc if custody failed. In base, close succeeded (closed row) even if custody failed. New: closed row only after custody+verify pass. Stricter. ✓.\n\nBut actually — is there any path where close succeeds while verifier fails? `run_verifier` failure kinds: verifier exit codes 0/2/3/4. vc != 0 → refused. vc == 0 → success. What if plumbline_verify.py itself is missing or crashes (exception)? subprocess.run would run python with the script; if the script raises, returncode 1 → vc=1 → refused. If the file is missing, python exits 2 with error → refused. Good.\n\nWhat about run_verifier's `rec` default — record path. After do_close's emit (no --out), record at default path. ✓.\n\nNow the `--allow-hand-times` interplay with the verifier: the verifier reads the allowance from the record, never from CLI. It recomputes hand times independently and requires the record's hand_times to match and the allowance block present when hand times exist. The close passes --allow-hand-times to the emitter only (a.allow_hand_times). The verifier \"still enforces the flags\" (per help text). So even with the switch, the verifier checks hand-time flagging integrity. Good.\n\nNow — an important check for rule 2: does `--allow-hand-times` in plumbline_job close/custody do anything beyond passing to emitter? `c.add_argument(\"--allow-hand-times\", ...)` and `k.add_argument(...)`. In do_close/do_custody, appended to emitter args only. ✓.\n\nNow rule 1 detailed re-scan for removed/loosened checks:\n\ncustody_history.py:\n- Removed the log-rewrite (fill_logger_times behavior). This REMOVED a mutation, not a check. The base flow: rewrite log so times present. New: refuse hand times. Stricter overall. But wait — could removing fill_logger_times remove a check? No; it was a writer, not a check.\n- read_log default ref: callers passing ref unchanged keep behavior. The verifier calls read_log with throwaway Refusals — meaning the verifier ignores log parse refusals? In read_calls: `calls = H.read_log(Path(log), H.Refusals(), str(seat))` — refusals go to a throwaway and are dropped. But that's within the verifier reading logs for recomputation; failures there would surface as mismatches (hand-time-unflagged / events). Not a loosening of emitter checks.\n\nemit_custody.py:\n- All context checks retained: cross_check for startedAt/producedAt/passes/controls, check_order, endpoint refusals, approval refusals, revision check, ledger-vs-inputs cross-checks (freeze, approvals, dispose, pin), pin checks, generation reply naming (in annotate, unshown but unchanged).\n- New refusals: hand-time. Stricter.\n- One subtle: `maker_history` signature gained `hand` param; behavior same otherwise.\n- `build` gained allow_hand param defaulting False — `main` passes a.allow_hand_times. Any other callers of build with allow_hand? Not shown; fine.\n\nWait, I should double check one potential issue: In `build`, the hand-time refusal is added AFTER `check_order` (line 839) and before returning. Refusals accumulate in `ref` regardless of order; main prints all and returns 3 if any. Order doesn't matter. Also — `if hand and not allow_hand: ref.add(...)` — this is inside build, reached always. ✓. No early return around it.\n\nAlso check: does the emitter still refuse \"nothing dated after emit time\" etc.? check_order unchanged (context). ✓.\n\nAnother subtle one: `events_log` — base: `ref.add(\"time\", f\"inputs/custody-events.jsonl line {n} is not JSON\")` — new: same with LEDGER_REL. ✓.\n\nNow — `writer_source` marks: for approve lines, base... base had no marks (no hand-time concept). New marks approval times from ledger lines with hand/absent writer as hand times → refusal unless switch. Stricter. ✓.\n\nPotential concern: `mark(f\"bar.approvals[{i}].at\", ...)` uses `live.get(\"_line\")` — fine.\n\nNow the disposal: base `elif dsp.get(\"at\")` etc. Also base: `xp[\"sources\"][\"disposition.at\"] = f\"inputs/custody-events.jsonl:{live['_line']}\"` when live — same.\n\nOne more thing in the emitter — line 833-834: `xp[\"times\"]` context. Fine.\n\n### plumbline_job.py — check for rule 1 violations\n\nBase do_close flow:\n```\n        log_row(job, \"closed\", {...})\n        print(\"\\njob closed: ...\")\n        if terminal: custody emit; if cc != 0: print CLOSED BUT ...; return cc\n        else: print no custody\n    return code\n```\n\nNew flow:\n```\n        if terminal:\n            custody emit (+switch); if cc: print; return cc\n            verify; if vc: print NOT CLOSED; log close_refused; return vc\n        log_row closed; print job closed\n        if not terminal: print no custody\n    return code\n```\n\nRule 1 concerns checks/refusals about custody history — none removed here. The closed row timing changed (after custody) — stricter.\n\nWait, actually there's something: base printed \"job closed\" and logged closed row, then custody failure returned cc (non-zero) — base close \"succeeded\" (closed row) even when custody failed. New: no closed row when custody/verify fails. Stricter, conforms.\n\ndo_custody: base just called emit_custody. New: emit (+switch), then verifier; return vc. The `custody` command isn't the close; rule 3 says \"the verifier cannot be bypassed at close\". do_close runs it. ✓.\n\nHmm — but wait: in do_close, is `run_verifier` guaranteed to run when the record write succeeded? cc == 0 → run verifier. What if `doc.get(\"terminal_state\") in TERMINALS` is False (non-terminal close) — base printed no custody; new prints no custody after closed row. No record to verify. ✓.\n\nOne question: `run_verifier` uses `getattr(a, \"custody\", None) or os.environ.get(\"PLUMBLINE_CUSTODY\")`. do_close's parser has `--custody`. If neither → cannot-run → close refused (exit 2). So close now REQUIRES a custody checker checkout for terminal closes. Stricter. Not a bypass.\n\n### custody_ledger.py — more scans for rule 1\n\n- `pin` refusal messages reworded; same refusals (host check, via check, already-records-calls check). ✓.\n- `approve`: same checks (freeze first, H.check_approval). ✓.\n- `freeze`: same refusals. ✓.\n- The final write block: base wrote ledger and custody always; new: custody only if ci_changed (freeze/approve/pin/dispose). show writes nothing (base: show returned before writes too — base `if a.cmd == \"show\": ... return 0` context). generation/evaluation/amendment/relay/spend don't modify ci. ✓.\n- `ev[\"writer\"] = writer_stamp()` — new stamp. Fine.\n- `dispose` event: base wrote only by/at; new adds decided_at/state/words. The emitter reads decided_at. Fine.\n\nNow — one more careful look at the emitter disposal cross-check for a potential downgrade. Base:\n\n```\n    if live and at and (live.get(\"at\") != at or (live.get(\"by\") and dsp.get(\"by\") and live.get(\"by\") != dsp.get(\"by\"))):\n        ref.add(\"time\", ...)\n```\n\nNew:\n\n```\n    live_at = (live.get(\"decided_at\") or live.get(\"at\")) if live else None\n    if live and at and (live_at != at or ...):\n        ref.add(\"time\", ...)\n```\n\nConsider an OLD-format ledger line (no decided_at): identical. Consider NEW line with decided_at=D ≠ at=T_tool, inputs at=D: new accepts (record at=D). In the base world, the ledger tool wrote inputs at=T_tool and lines without decided_at, so this exact input state couldn't arise. The new tool writes inputs at=decided_at (line 326: `disp = {..., \"at\": decided_at}`), consistent. So no base-constructed input now passes that previously failed. And the ledger tool bounds decided_at between last check and clock (new checks). Verdict: new feature, conforming.\n\nBUT — hold on. Is `disposition.at = decided_at` a hand-entered time that the emitter does NOT flag as a hand time, while the emitter DOES flag other hand-entered times (inputs-no-ledger)? The mark for disposition.at: `if live: src = writer_source(live); if src: mark(...)` — tool-written line → no mark → not a hand time. The line contains decided_at which the operator typed. But the line was tool-stamped, and the tool checked decided_at (parseable, >= last check, <= clock, source required). Per the rule's definition — \"times no tool stamped\" — the tool did stamp this event (with both its clock and the declared time). The emitter's hand-time concept (HAND TIMES doc, lines 24-30): \"a ledger line with writer: hand or no writer, a time typed into inputs/custody.json with no ledger line behind it, or a server-set time from a harness log call with no logger stamp\". decided_at has a ledger line behind it, tool-written. So by the change's own definition it's not a hand time. The rule says the switch affects only hand-time refusals — it doesn't say every declared time must be flagged. And rule 1: no loosened check — the disposition cross-check remains; the ledger tool adds bounds. I'll accept.\n\nHmm, but let me reconsider whether the emitter's disposition handling could mask a hand-entered inputs time. Case: no live dispose line; inputs disposition.at = X (typed). New: `elif dsp.get(\"at\"): mark(\"disposition.at\", rel_inputs, ..., \"inputs-no-ledger\")` → hand time → refused unless switch. Base: no refusal (X compared only via check_order presumably). Stricter. ✓.\n\nAnd with live line + decided_at: inputs at=D written BY THE TOOL, line tool-written. Consistent. OK.\n\n### Now the verifier — deeper rule 3 scan\n\n\"no verifier failure is skipped when a field is absent\":\n\n- main: schema first; if xp missing → fail. ✓\n- check_events: absent arrays → fail (got None != expected list). ✓ Actually wait: `if got != expected` — if xp has generations = [] and expected = [] → pass (correct). Absent → None != [] → fail. ✓\n- check_spend: absent → fail. ✓\n- check_hand_times: flags absent → return [] — hmm. But when would flags be absent while recomp non-empty? Only if the schema permits omitting hand_times. The emitter always writes it. The schema validates `required`. I can't see the schema. Risky but plausible-required. Also check_shadow would catch hand_times mismatch when present. Given the emitter writes hand_times always, and the rule's concern is about the verifier skipping failures — the design here: hand_times absent → check_hand_times can't compare → but schema (which requires the field per the emitter's invariant) fails. I'll accept.\n\nHmm, actually, wait. Let me reconsider `check_hand_times`'s early returns:\n\n```\n    xp = ... if isinstance(record, dict) else None\n    if not isinstance(xp, dict):\n        return fails\n    flags = xp.get(\"hand_times\")\n    if not isinstance(flags, list):\n        return fails\n```\n\nIf hand_times present but not a list → no hand-time fails... but schema would fail (type). Since main returns on schema failures before these checks, non-list hand_times never reaches here. OK.\n\n- check_shadow: `for key in OWNED: if key not in derived or key not in xp: continue` — if xp lacks a key the emitter always writes (e.g., times, schema), shadow skips but schema presumably requires. For keys like \"verdict\" — derived only sets verdict if s5 record readable; `if key not in derived ... continue` — verdict absent from derived → skip. That's by design (verdict may be absent). Not a bypass of a mandated check.\n\n- The snapshot before/after: detects writes during verification → \"writes\" failure. Fine.\n\n\"the verifier cannot be bypassed at close\": do_close runs it; failure → close_refused + return code. Also `--allow-hand-times` doesn't weaken the verifier (no switch). ✓\n\nAlso note: do_close passes `a` to run_verifier, which reads `a.custody`. ✓.\n\nOne more: could close bypass the verifier by the emitter failing with cc==3? Then return cc — close refused too (no closed row). Stricter than base.\n\nWait, actually — hmm, in the base, when custody failed, the job was still CLOSED (closed row logged, \"job closed\" printed) and exit non-zero. Now the closed row is not logged when custody or verify fails. Is that \"removing\" anything? No — it's tightening the close. Rule allows stricter.\n\n### Now let me look for any sneaky violation I might have missed.\n\nThe `_find_pin_in` function has odd logic:\n\n```\n                if vv.startswith(\"aaeb7e05\") and vv.endswith(\"bcd18\"):\n                    return vv\n```\n\nThis accepts any hex string starting with \"aaeb7e05\" and ending with \"bcd18\" as a pin match — regardless of the actual checker sha. Hmm, that's a hardcoded prefix/suffix. Wait, this looks like it could match a specific known sha (aaeb7e05...bcd18 — 8+5 hex, maybe a 64-hex with that prefix/suffix). This is in find_pin, which finds the pinned sha to compare against the checker's actual sha. If the pin.json contains a value like \"aaeb7e05...bcd18\", it's accepted as the pin. This doesn't weaken the check — file_sha(check) != pin → exit 2. The odd matcher just tolerates formats. It could be a backdoor? If someone crafts pin.json with a string \"aaeb7e05\" + junk + \"bcd18\", then pin = that string, and file_sha(check) (64 hex) would equal it only if the checker's sha literally equals that string. file_sha returns hexdigest (64 lowercase hex). The pin returned would be the \"vv\" string found — comparison `file_sha(check) != pin` — so the pin must equal the actual sha for the check to pass. The weird matcher returns vv only if vv starts/ends with those — but it still must equal the actual file sha. So no bypass; it just allows pin.json to omit the full sha?? No — it must match the actual sha exactly. So harmless. Weird, but not a violation of the stated rule (it's about verifier checks on the record; this is the checker-pin integrity, and it remains enforced: sha must match).\n\nHmm wait, actually there's something: `if vv.startswith(\"aaeb7e05\") and vv.endswith(\"bcd18\"): return vv` — if pin.json contains garbage \"aaeb7e05zzzzbcd18\", pin = that; then `file_sha(check) != pin` → True → exit 2 (cannot run). Fails closed. OK.\n\nNow `standard_check`: runs the pinned checker; non-zero → 3 (fail). ✓.\n\n`WRITER_FORM` — allowance writer must be kit/*.py@12hex, never \"hand\". ✓.\n\nNow let me reconsider rule 2 once more with a fine-tooth comb, particularly `flag_calls` and `hand_time_entries` marking — under `--allow-hand-times`, hand times are written into the record and allowed. Do any of these hand times feed checks that would otherwise refuse? E.g., a call with no logger stamp: startedAt comes from Date header; cross_check compares inputs' startedAt vs started — still enforced. check_order uses the record times — enforced. So order checks use hand times as inputs but the order check itself still runs. The rule says the switch \"must not skip, weaken or bypass any other check (order, cross-checks against logs, endpoint, approval, revision)\" — the checks still run on the (hand) values. ✓.\n\nEdge: with the switch, does the emitter allow a hand time that CONTRADICTS the log? The cross-check refusals are separate and still active. ✓.\n\nNow, is there anything where a refusal was converted to a gap? Let me scan the emitter diff for ref→gaps changes... The `...` elisions make this hard, but the shown hunks don't convert any ref.add to gaps.add. The gaps at 975-979 (missing sent_at/received_at → gaps) — is that context or new? Lines 975-977 shown with \" \" (context) — wait:\n\n```\n   975       if gaps is not None and any(\"sent_at\" in c.raw or \"received_at\" in c.raw for c in calls):\n   976           for c in calls:\n   977               missing = [k for k in (\"sent_at\", \"received_at\") if not c.raw.get(k)]\n   ...\n   981       annotate(calls, \"maker\", ...)\n```\n\nContext — unchanged. Hmm, but now with hand-time refusals added, calls missing stamps are BOTH gaps and hand-time refusals. Stricter. Fine.\n\nNow — wait, there's something I should check: in maker_history, `calls = [c for c in calls if c.at]` — context (unchanged). startedAt from first.started... `first = min(calls, key=lambda c: c.started)` — hmm, `c.started` for calls without sent_at = created/date. Unchanged behavior.\n\nOK — now, the one thing I keep coming back to as the riskiest for a \"DOES_NOT_CONFORM\" verdict: the disposition decided_at change, and the hand_times absence in the verifier. Let me weigh once more.\n\nThe rule text: \"1. No cross-check or refusal is loosened, removed or downgraded (for example: a wider tolerance, a refusal turned into a warning or a gap, a check that is skipped, returned from early, or no longer reached).\"\n\nThe disposition cross-check: is it \"loosened\"? Base compared inputs' at to ledger `at`. New compares to ledger `decided_at or at`. For tool-written lines with decided_at, the record's disposition.at = decided_at — a time the tool did not stamp with its clock but bounded. The ledger line itself is the source and carries both times. The cross-check \"the ledger vs the inputs\" is preserved verbatim in structure. The change also keeps: `if live and at and (... live.get(\"by\") != dsp.get(\"by\"))` refusal. So no refusal removed. The value compared changed, but consistently with the new ledger feature which itself enforces bounds (decided_at >= last check, <= clock, requires source). This is \"new fields, new checks\" — allowed. Also F1b mentioned in comments — seems like an intended feature of this change set.\n\nAlso consider: does the new `dispose --decided-at` allow a hand-entered time to enter the record WITHOUT --allow-hand-times? Yes — decided_at becomes disposition.at. Is that a violation of rule 2? Rule 2 constrains `--allow-hand-times`; it doesn't say \"all hand times must go through the switch\". Rule 1 constrains loosening of EXISTING cross-checks/refusals. The base emitter had no refusal for hand-entered disposition times (there was no such refusal — the base tool always stamped disposition.at itself; a hand-typed inputs disposition.at with no ledger line was... base: `elif dsp.get(\"at\")` source path, no refusal). New: refused as hand time. So relative to base, hand-entered disposition times are now MORE controlled. decided_at is a new, bounded, tool-recorded path. I'm fairly confident the intended verdict is CONFORMS.\n\nNow, another candidate: `read_log`'s new optional `ref` — the verifier uses throwaway Refusals, so log-contradiction refusals (created vs Date > TOLERANCE) inside read_log are DROPPED by the verifier. But the verifier isn't the emitter; rule 1 concerns the emitter's checks. The emitter's read_log calls pass its real ref (maker_history line 967: `log_calls = H.read_log(log, ref, \"maker\")` — yes, passes ref). ✓.\n\nHmm, wait — in hand_time_set (verifier), read_log with throwaway — fine.\n\nWhat about `pin_for` — unchanged. `annotate`, `check_pin_order`, `link_call` — not shown/uncontext; assume unchanged.\n\nLet me also double-check `flag_calls` doesn't skip when hand is None: `if hand is None: return` — hand is always a dict in build (line 328). In maker_history, `hand` param — build passes `hand` dict. OK. But note: `hand_time_entries` only reports entries when sent is None or received is None — and requires created or date. A call with NEITHER created nor date → read_log adds the \"cannot be read\" refusal (context line 247). ✓.\n\nNow, plumbline_job close: `if doc.get(\"terminal_state\") in TERMINALS:` — and within, after custody ok, `vc, kind = run_verifier(job, a)`. Note: run_verifier is called with the job's default record path. But do_close's emit used cargs without --out, so record at default. ✓.\n\nOne more possible issue: In do_close, base returned `cc` after printing \"CLOSED, BUT...\" — new same. And new returns vc on verify fail. So close exits non-zero when verify fails. ✓ Rule 3.\n\nAnd \"no verifier failure is skipped when a field is absent\" — hmm, let me look again at `check_events`'s generation open/close: opens without closes → fail. ✓. Relay-as-approval ✓. Spend settle labels ✓.\n\nWhat about the schema check `check_schema` — `if not Path(schema_path).is_file(): return [schema failure]` ✓ fails closed.\n\n`main`: `if not record.is_file(): return finish(2, ...)` — exit 2, close refused. ✓.\n\nOK now — one more scan of emit_custody for the switch: `build(..., allow_hand=a.allow_hand_times)`. And `if hand and not allow_hand: ref.add(\"hand-time\", ...)`. Note the refusal happens at the END of build — after check_order etc. All refusals collected in ref; main returns 3 if ref non-empty. There's no path where allow_hand skips earlier refusals. ✓.\n\nAlso `xp[\"allowances\"]` only when hand and allow_hand. Verifier: if recomp non