                "text": "Let me work through this carefully.\n\nThe question: does the change conform to the rule?\n\nRULE:\n1. No cross-check or refusal is loosened, removed or downgraded (wider tolerance, refusal→warning/gap, check skipped, early return, no longer reached).\n2. `--allow-hand-times`, if present, affects ONLY the refusal of hand-entered times. Must not skip, weaken, bypass any other check (order, cross-checks against logs, endpoint, approval, revision).\n3. If `kit/plumbline_verify.py` is in the change: no verifier failure is skipped when a field is absent, and the verifier cannot be bypassed at close (close must not succeed when the verifier fails). If it's not in the change, point 3 doesn't apply.\n\nLet me examine the changes in each file.\n\n### kit/custody_history.py\n\nRemoved: `fill_logger_times` actual implementation — previously it rewrote the harness log to add `sent_at`/`received_at` derived from response `created`/`Date` header. Now it's a no-op returning False (deprecated). The docstring at top changed accordingly: previously a call with no logger times would be \"brought forward once, in place and in its own format\" — now a call with no logger times is a hand time: emitter refuses by default, or lists it under `--allow-hand-times`.\n\nIs this a loosening? Previously, the log was rewritten so the emitter would read `sent_at`/`received_at` from the log (derived from Date header). Now, the emitter reads them as hand times and refuses them by default. That's stricter (a new refusal), not looser. The old behavior wrote into the log; the new behavior refuses. So stricter. Good.\n\n`read_log` now takes optional `ref` and `who` (default None / \"log\"). If ref is None, uses a throwaway Refusals. Previously `ref` was required. Does this loosen anything? The callers in emit_custody pass their own Refusals, so unchanged. `read_log` internal checks remain: the `ref.add` for missing time, and the self-contradiction check with TOLERANCE_S. Let me check lines 247-251:\n\n```\n   247               ref.add(\"time\", f\"{who}: {c.label} carries neither a response Date header nor a `created` time; \"\n   248                               f\"its time cannot be read from the log\")\n   249           elif c.created and c.date and (c.created - c.date).total_seconds() > TOLERANCE_S:\n   250               ref.add(\"time\", ...)\n```\n\nThese are context lines, unchanged. TOLERANCE_S unchanged (60 s at base; nothing changed it).\n\nRemoved `_clock` helper — fine, it was only used by fill_logger_times.\n\nAdded `hand_time_entries()` — new function, fine.\n\nSo custody_history.py: no loosening; actually new refusal (hand times). Good.\n\nWait — but the removal of `fill_logger_times` — previously, before the change, a log with no logger times would be brought forward so the emitter would NOT refuse... Actually wait. Let me think about the base behavior. In the base, `fill_logger_times(log)` was called before `read_log`, so calls got logger times written into the log; then `read_log` reads them; `Call.started`/`Call.at` would use sent_at/received_at. Cross-checks against the harness log then compare hand-entered times to these derived-from-Date-header times.\n\nIn the new version, `fill_logger_times` is a no-op, so calls without logger times are read as hand times: `Call.started` = created or date, `Call.at` = date or created. The cross-checks still run (H.cross_check on startedAt, producedAt, passes.at, controls.at). And additionally the emitter now refuses hand times by default (new refusal kind `hand-time`). Under `--allow-hand-times`, hand times are allowed and listed.\n\nHmm — but wait: under `--allow-hand-times`, are the cross-checks still run? Yes: in maker_history, `H.cross_check(ref, \"startedAt\", ci.get(\"startedAt\"), started, ...)` still runs regardless. The hand-time flagging is separate. Let me verify the order in build():\n\n```\n   841 +     # ---- the versioned block, hand times, the F1 arrays, spend and the inputs' own x-plumbline ----\n   842 +     xp[\"schema\"] = \"plumbline/x-plumbline/v1\"\n   843 +     xp[\"hand_times\"] = [hand[k] for k in hand]\n   844 +     if hand and allow_hand:\n   845 +         xp[\"allowances\"] = ...\n   846 +                                            \"at\": H.iso(now)}}\n   847 +     if hand and not allow_hand:\n   848 +         for e in hand.values():\n   849 +             ref.add(\"hand-time\", f\"{e['field']} {e['file']}:{e['line']} ({e['source']})\")\n```\n\nSo without the switch, hand times → refusals. With the switch → allowance recorded. And all other checks (check_order, cross_check, endpoint, approval, revision) run as before. The switch affects only the hand-time refusal. Good for rule 2 so far.\n\nBut wait — I need to check: does `--allow-hand-times` affect anything else? In plumbline_job.py close: `cargs.append(\"--allow-hand-times\")` — just passes to emitter. In do_custody: same. The verifier doesn't take a switch. OK.\n\nOne thing to check in rule 2: \"It must not skip, weaken or bypass any other check (order, cross-checks against logs, endpoint, approval, revision).\" The emitter's `build` with allow_hand=True: does it skip any cross-checks? Looking at the code — cross-checks run unconditionally. `check_order(pred, packet, ci, now, ref)` runs unconditionally (context line 839). Revision check runs unconditionally (line 334). Approval checks run unconditionally (line 391). Endpoint checks — in annotate/pin_for — unconditional. So the switch only gates the hand-time refusal. Good.\n\n### kit/custody_ledger.py\n\nThis adds new subcommands (generation, evaluation, amendment, relay, spend), writer stamps, RefusingParser, dispose with --decided-at, etc.\n\nCheck whether any refusal is loosened. The dispose behavior changed:\n\nBase:\n```\n    else:\n        ci[\"disposition\"] = {**(ci.get(\"disposition\") or {}), \"by\": a.by, \"at\": t}\n```\nand event `{\"event\": \"dispose\", \"by\": a.by, \"at\": t}`.\n\nNew: dispose with optional --decided-at. The emitter's cross-check for disposition changed:\n\nBase (emit_custody):\n```\n    if live and at and (live.get(\"at\") != at or (live.get(\"by\") and dsp.get(\"by\") and live.get(\"by\") != dsp.get(\"by\"))):\n        ref.add(\"time\", f\"disposition: the ledger says {dsp.get('by')!r} at {at!r} but inputs/custody-events.jsonl logged ...\")\n    if live and not at and live.get(\"at\"):\n        at = live.get(\"at\")\n```\n\nNew:\n```\n    live_at = (live.get(\"decided_at\") or live.get(\"at\")) if live else None\n    if live and at and (live_at != at or (live.get(\"by\") and dsp.get(\"by\") and live.get(\"by\") != dsp.get(\"by\"))):\n        ref.add(\"time\", ...)\n    if live and not at and live_at:\n        at = live_at\n```\n\nSo the emitter now compares against decided_at if present. Is this a loosening? The ledger line now carries both `at` (tool clock) and `decided_at` (the hand-declared time, bounded by checks in custody_ledger: decided_at >= last logged check, <= tool clock, and requires decided_source). The emitter's record carries decided_at as disposition.at. Hmm.\n\nIs this a loosening of a cross-check? The base cross-check compared inputs' disposition.at against the ledger's `at` (tool-stamped). Now, if the ledger line has decided_at, the emitter compares inputs' at against decided_at, and the record's disposition.at = decided_at. So a hand-declared time (decided_at) can become the record's disposition.at, where previously the record would carry the tool-stamped `at`.\n\nBut wait — is decided_at a \"hand time\"? In the emitter's hand-time detection: `writer_source(live)` — the dispose event carries `writer: \"kit/custody_ledger.py@...\"` (written by the tool at line 492: `ev[\"writer\"] = writer_stamp()`). So `writer_source` returns None (a tool) — the dispose line is tool-stamped, so disposition.at marked from the ledger is NOT a hand time even when decided_at is present. Hmm, so a hand-entered decided_at (typed by a human