s that all of them are flagged. That's checking completeness of flags, not verifying the flagged values.\n\n`flag-without-source` (line 85): \"a flag in `x-plumbline.hand_times[]` has no hand line behind it\" — checks each flag corresponds to a real hand time.\n\nSo the flags are checked for honesty (you can't flag things that aren't hand times, you can't omit hand times), but the time values themselves are not verified against anything. Does the document say or imply that a flagged time is checked/verified/trustworthy?\n\nHmm, \"The listing is not disclosure-only\" — this phrase could be concerning. It says the listing (of hand times) is not merely disclosure; it's enforced. But what's enforced is that the flags are complete and accurate as flags, not that the times are correct. Does this \"imply the opposite\" of rule 2? Rule 2 says the switch \"only FLAGS hand-entered times (records them as such); it never verifies them\". The document explicitly says \"The switch flags hand times and never verifies them. `--allow-hand-times` does not check the time against a log or a clock.\" (line 141-142). That's a direct statement of rule 2's first part.\n\nThe \"not disclosure-only\" part: the verifier enforces that all hand times are flagged and flags correspond to actual hand times, and that the allowance metadata is present. This doesn't present flagged times as verified — it presents the flagging itself as enforced. I think this is consistent: the switch only flags; the flags are then enforced to be complete/honest. A flagged time is still marked as a hand time in the record — it's not presented as checked.\n\nDoes the document present a flagged time as trustworthy anywhere? Line 31-32: \"`hand_times[]` — every hand time the record carries, each `{field, file, line, source}`\". The record carries hand_times with source info — it's transparent about them being hand times. The allowance block records the switch, writer, at — metadata of the allowance, not a verification.\n\nLine 146-147: \"The switch silences nothing else — a repo overlay whose log contradicts the hand time still exits 3 with the switch on.\" Wait, this is interesting. \"a repo overlay whose log contradicts the hand time still exits 3 with the switch on\". Hmm — so if a log contradicts the hand time, it exits 3 even with the switch on? But the switch \"does not check the time against a log or a clock\" (line 141-142)?\n\nWait, let me re-read. Line 173 (`time` kind): \"under `--allow-hand-times`, a hand time is more than 60 s off its harness call (the switch silences nothing else)\". Hmm, so under the switch, if a hand time is more than 60s off its harness call, it's refused with kind `time`. But line 105-106 says hand times for passes/controls are hand times \"even when a call is linked\" — and the cross-check \"within 60 s\" happens: \"Cross-checked against the log within 60 s\".\n\nSo the document says: the switch does not verify the time against a log or clock (line 141-142), BUT there's a separate 60-second cross-check against harness logs that still applies (lines 98-99, 103, 105-106, 116-119, 173). Hmm — \"A logged time is still cross-checked against any typed value within 60 s\" (line 98).\n\nIs this a contradiction of rule 2? Rule 2: \"The `--allow-hand-times` switch only FLAGS hand-entered times (records them as such); it never verifies them\". The 60s cross-check is... a verification of the hand time? Hmm. Let me think.\n\nLine 97-98: \"with `--allow-hand-times` flagged in `x-plumbline.hand_times[]` and never verified (see \"Hand times\" above). A logged time is still cross-checked against any typed value within 60 s.\"\n\nSo the document says hand times are \"never verified\" and then immediately says typed values are cross-checked against logged times within 60s. And line 173: under the switch, a hand time more than 60s off its harness call is refused (kind `time`).\n\nHmm, is that \"verifying\" the hand time? The document frames it as: the switch never verifies the hand time itself (doesn't check it against a log or clock at flagging time); separately, any cross-check that applies to all times (the 60s tolerance against harness logs) still applies. So a hand time for `startedAt` that's more than 60s off the harness log is refused as a `time` mismatch even with the switch on. That IS a form of checking the hand time against a log...\n\nWait, but that seems contradictory within the document itself: line 141-142 says \"`--allow-hand-times` does not check the time against a log or a clock\" but line 173 says under the switch, a hand time more than 60s off its harness call is refused. Hmm, actually no — let me re-read line 173: \"`time` | under `--allow-hand-times`, a hand time is more than 60 s off its harness call (the switch silences nothing else)\". \n\nHmm wait, but which hand times have harness calls? Passes and controls can be linked to calls; startedAt/producedAt come from the maker log. bar.frozenAt, approvals, disposition have \"no harness log\". So for those fields with harness logs, the 60s cross-check applies even with the switch.\n\nBut hold on — line 105: \"`passes.<id>.at` ... a hand time even when a call is linked (`inputs-no-ledger`): refused by default, flagged under the switch, never verified; cross-checked within 60 s\". So it says \"never verified; cross-checked within 60 s\". The document's own distinction: \"never verified\" means the switch doesn't validate the hand time as correct; the cross-check is a bounded sanity check (60s tolerance) against log-derived values that applies regardless.\n\nHmm, this is tricky for judging rule 2. Rule 2 says the switch \"never verifies them, and a flagged time is not presented as checked, verified or trustworthy\". The document explicitly states \"never verified\" multiple times (lines 97, 103, 105, 106, 109, 141). The cross-check within 60s is a separate emission-time consistency check — the emitter refuses a hand time that contradicts a log by more than 60s